[{"id":"VRCE-6-26-1","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":1,"english":"That captor of hostile capitals, the mightarmed Rāma, was advancing (upon Larikā) with the sounds of drums mixed with the blares of conchs.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.653,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026001","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"66f561e4a068d2498afe17a7f723d7abf6f1faa86f9925ab74e26b9325e50dfb","compact":"5e880f3fdb4cfd3f385d53e5f292ce4a6ffaaaea4ecf30fa3ec32c278b80ade4","nasal":"eb9ed91b26c21ab2614952b0359c230b094cb4c7357db9ee41173272db156518"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-2","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":2,"english":"Hearing this noise, Ravana-lord of Raks asas-reflecting for a while, cast his eyes upon his counsellors.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026002","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b185213199688876d9229f24b2e8bff35b0ae850fc917e0df3733c1b45bd2b62","compact":"f29e65db9b4e581951d7d06bb91f4048f7ec50afd7049f93b25aa92e4ba0a619","nasal":"f29e65db9b4e581951d7d06bb91f4048f7ec50afd7049f93b25aa92e4ba0a619"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-3","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":3,"english":"Addressing there all those counsellors, that afflictor of the world—the crooked and highly powerful lord of the Rākşasas, Rāvaņa, making his court resound, running down (Rāma's) crossing the main, as well as his vigour, strength and manhood, said to them.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026003","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"082fae8ef6576e3b8d761f0980338b10b0a1951de9bb7a8fb8353db9e728031e","compact":"b323838b3016f34bb2b15c40f8897b2c1811de848333a97dde80b5e64a8ea307","nasal":"b323838b3016f34bb2b15c40f8897b2c1811de848333a97dde80b5e64a8ea307"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-4","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":4,"english":"What you have said touching Rama's (prowess), has been heard by me. And I also know you to be of sterling valour in the field. Yet why, knowing Rāma's prowess, do you silently look at each other?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.598,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026004","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ac1a966fa5322c8dd4626d50abaaef8eeb454fbf1afbad1c79ba337543663d8d","compact":"6e4ee35ed9b407f646131466b3627048aa3192569c762f4a6683059c3b4fc2c3","nasal":"d209acd0f6a307afa8e4c41f6ce0d864d29fe03848006671d063b8caa2509c01"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-5","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":5,"english":"Then the maternal grand sire (of Rāvaņa), possessed of exceeding wisdom, named Malyavān, hearing his words, said.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.794,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026005","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"79b608926b4d68fd7a65c791e56a3a67ac3a848cd7a89136987f1db028b9451d","compact":"4ec1f9fdf9fe15298d37a747b6d3654278ef8c83074cb5458049562069c60a7b","nasal":"c6105e2bc5108767fc1d44e273aa3933cf37f6ccb22b5730fd370fdaa17bffa5"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-6","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":6,"english":"The king that is accomplished in the various kinds of learning,* and that follow polity, ever command affluence and bring his enemies under his subjection. *The commentator says there are four and ten kinds of learning; but unfortunately he does not enumerate them.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.742,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026006","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7a3ff6b378f23775319539ec18a3f5a7bac3937ac8542263322af1d1c6a43600","compact":"52352b91a30f9e0558ac1a0f2b852b6b130bf508ac78d17f9d45640a782a6281","nasal":"e9c7676f2e034a738216637e6a33e51553137c46cf8bf2a029720d8b0e8b94cc"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-7","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":7,"english":"He that seasonably concludes peace and carries on war with his enemies, and who advances his own party, attain great affluence.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.762,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026007","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5505740905adab22b2b30fd017cab5ecd6be33226adc3d7d53620d0cc7a4358f","compact":"6c64bbe0014c7d2be8e0a238ed7809daf157da3ee7782d6d33de4e8adb43c552","nasal":"6c64bbe0014c7d2be8e0a238ed7809daf157da3ee7782d6d33de4e8adb43c552"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-8","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":8,"english":"A king should enter into peace, if he be an equal or inferior of his adversary, and engage in war, if be more powerful, but an enemy should never be disergarded.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026008","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e49b0dcadf09ad174474dd6692636a25ee172f11d5b06e55cada1344c3e3b8fe","compact":"d7eca876b5a326c60fc7ebbb779dfbd24cf69cf260a0e7b55910303b4856254f","nasal":"d7eca876b5a326c60fc7ebbb779dfbd24cf69cf260a0e7b55910303b4856254f"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-9","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":9,"english":"Therefore, O Rāvana, peace with Rāma is what commends itself to me; and do you render to him that for which you have been invaded (by him), viz., Sītā.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.797,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026009","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"307c9b15e9274ba30e1d218190034ff849f21c32b41148bc27e0755cd99fd803","compact":"d37d9e1b3b301c345c7d7a7f37d8826677f4fced4b8627bd2c1010095e410f47","nasal":"d37d9e1b3b301c345c7d7a7f37d8826677f4fced4b8627bd2c1010095e410f47"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-10","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":10,"english":"All the Devarsis and Gandharvas wish for (Rāma's) victory. Therefore, do not enter into hostilities with him; but do you bend your mind to the conclusion of peace with him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026010","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2fb1c39ad66e50e74f08c93a467f51edc747e1284bf89a99f657e7e72e4098e3","compact":"2c71e4fd7c07437082c491361d745e94edf240655875e364414d715923af32f4","nasal":"8b12b64bbffb50b3d6912ad6bc0956b0e99d4f955cf84f703eece2c97d0320d7"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-11","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":11,"english":"That possessor of the six attributes, the Greatfather, has appointed two courses, (respectively) for the Asuras and the celestials, having for their eousness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026011","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"be9b1163b7722b97ed18095b192592ce8f38a2ab5ffce8ee688dbabee88d31fa","compact":"3646cfe729450839ebab3208741fdf0eaf550505ad9adc435795cf7f4d78a11e","nasal":"3646cfe729450839ebab3208741fdf0eaf550505ad9adc435795cf7f4d78a11e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-12","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":12,"english":"Righteousness, we hear, is the course of the high-souled celestials, as, O. Rākşasa, unrighteousness is that of Rākşasa and Asuras.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.597,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026012","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d497685e84b05629d2559f3b2c7db5c4efce45b5e09255778ea43fc83bedaadb","compact":"03d2a52d6217d1cbedf053bf220feca8ac6dcad7717a25d4047fe5f177255e77","nasal":"03d2a52d6217d1cbedf053bf220feca8ac6dcad7717a25d4047fe5f177255e77"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-13","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":13,"english":"During the Kţta Yuga, righteousness swallow up sin; and during Tisya,* unrighteousness swallow up virtue. *The Kali Yuga.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.508,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026013","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1b9a5d156c9b273ff4c26711ee649f7d6c94565ad6376776968408204b2900a0","compact":"bd2eed7a53e0c53117941bcd3d21b4bf983f765c86ad0d517f8d228e1de14048","nasal":"bd2eed7a53e0c53117941bcd3d21b4bf983f765c86ad0d517f8d228e1de14048"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-14","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":14,"english":"You, ranging the three worlds, worlds, had extinguished pregnant piety, and reaped unrighteousness; and it is for this that your foes have waxed potent. *While ranging the three worlds on a career of conquest, you didst, disturbing sacrifices, etc., wrong righteousness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.597,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026014","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"59a565e261cc80460ed09325e821024826e3817c5ab5e7bf46f19f40522d5b3f","compact":"54673c1b57d09085130504b2242ddb0a4b80014988e8781b067dd6b4f34503a1","nasal":"eb6623d77058f2ac3b11f7ab04b5f6d41b9c831b267a63a5aba6ea8379fae007"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-15","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":15,"english":"Having grown through through your your neglect, unrighteousness devour us, and that protector of the gods, (righteousness), is strengthening the side of the celestials.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.825,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026015","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b2d61c1acd19245b19b9a0ff5064efde5f3308193794864d61f36a9342b564df","compact":"aac94d33845ce654fc0edb7e030f5a812beb3a55cc28de84f614e62dff62a5cd","nasal":"aac94d33845ce654fc0edb7e030f5a812beb3a55cc28de84f614e62dff62a5cd"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-16","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":26,"verse":16,"english":"You, addicted to carnal pleasures and acting in defiance of duty, had aroused the high wrath of the saints resembling fire. The power of these possessed of souls sanctified through asceticism, and ever engaged in invoking the aid of righteousness, is incapable of being resisted and is like flaming fire.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.564,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-35-16","DUTT-6-35-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6026016","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0b9f019e4dc45edc039e6b8300572d5e2ddd660a779859386fc5867764e97a3b","compact":"df924c88403d8304babb60eeabf049df3016c00e1d0083ee0cb45eafa2760617","nasal":"4a24c947b7f6119a8eb77627778f88279c6b47f9ac6afb71b96d79f4ae5e6308"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-26-17","kanda":